Pure Quartz, which is also known as Rock Crystal, is colorless. Various impurities are responsible for the extensive range of colors. The main crystalline Quartz varieties used as gemstones are described below.
Am[ethyst], the purple variety, is the most popular and valuable Quartz gemstone. Am[ethyst] ranges from light to dark purple. See the Am[ethyst] gemstone page for more details.
Citrine is the yellow, orange, or reddish-brown variety of Quartz. It is usually[ colored] by heat treatment of Am[ethyst] or Sm[oky] Quartz. Light yellow or lemon yellow Citrine is often called Lemon Quartz in the gem trade. See the Citrine gemstone page for more details.
