> The over[payment] scam is another time-honored classic that's made a[ successful] leap to the online world. It goes like this: A business (usually a small,[ seemingly vulnerable] one)[ receives] a[ check] from a buyer, usually of a big-[ticket] item. But[ suddenly] there's a hitch — the buyer has[ mistakenly] written the[ check] for more than the[ correct] amount. To[ avoid] the trouble of writing another[ check], the buyer[ asks] that the[ check] be deposited and the difference wired back to them. We all know where this is going, right? The[ check bounces], the wire transfer is gone, the scammer is untraceable and the business owner is also responsible for bank fees.
Over[payment] scams are[ pretty easy] to[ avoid] with just a few safeguards. There are so many warning signs with these cons that you should be[ able] to stop things before they start. A no-check policy would[ instantly solve] the problem, and it's also best to steer clear of wire transfers. If you must accept[ checks] for some reason, always ask for all of the buyer's contact information and then double-check — or, better[ yet], deal with them in person and ask for a check from a local bank. Wait for[ checks] to clear before you use any of the funds and, above all, never accept a[ check] for more than the agreed-[upon] amount.

